  "evidence": {
    "status": "qualified",
    "conditions": "The immutable Linux/ARM64 image ran on four directly connected NVIDIA GB10 systems at TP4/DCP4 with FP8 KV, 24 GiB per rank, a 1,048,576-token request limit, 8,192 scheduler tokens, interval two, BF16 DFlash2 depth seven, SparkCache tail-cow-v2 publication, and a 300-second shared-prefix lease.",
    "measurement": "The page-tail source completed exact 131,072, 262,144, 524,288, and 921,600-token growth. The registry image then repeated DFlash warmup after docker restart and served concurrent requests over the 921,600-token stored prefix, including during image-transfer pressure.",
    "result": "Every growth step returned its exact codeword and remained a page delta. The 921,600-token root used a 7,459-byte manifest with three delta stages. After restart, two concurrent requests returned exact responses with no post-readiness JIT, CUDA error, or stuck request.",
    "conclusion": "The registry artifact starts the preferred DCP4 profile, incrementally publishes growing contexts, restores them after restart, and withholds Docker readiness until DFlash warmup completes.",
    "limitations": [
      "The built-image smoke did not repeat video input or persistent multimodal restore after a process restart.",
      "DCP1 and DCP2 are implemented profiles, but asynchronous capture is disabled because those slot sizes lack matching live evidence.",
      "The original cuBLAS failure was observed once; readiness warmup removes the reproduced first-use JIT condition but does not prove it was the only possible cause."
